  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000122388,
      "SummaryText": "Home Market Foods operates a refrigeration system utilizing anhydrous ammonia as the primary refrigerant. Mechanical refrigeration is a thermodynamic process where the refrigerant, ammonia, is used as a heat exchanging fluid to maintain a desired temperature in an enclosed environment. The refrigeration cycle consists of evaporation, compression, condensation and expansion. Evaporation and condensation are designed to alter the quantity of energy associated with the refrigerant. Compression and expansion are designed to change the availability of the associated energy. The refrigeration cycle is a closed-loop process. The ammonia cycle utilizes fluid temperature, pressure and latent heat of vaporization for cooling. The refrigeration system uses this phenomenon to remove heat from a desired space, and then to discard that heat through condensation. Home Market Foods operates its two (2) main machine room (having compressors, condenser, receivers), as well as the low pressure side of the system (pump receivers, coil evaporators, water chillers and coolers) to maintain processing, freezer and holding room temperatures required for manufacturing of and storage of their product. There are approximately 10 employees that work at the Home Market Foods facility, including hourly and salary personnel that support manufacturing and logistics relating to product preparation, shipment, and cold storage. Home Market Foods operates approximately 24 hours a day, five days a week. Aggregate ammonia storage is 27,700 pounds (lbs), consisting of two separate systems with capacity of 15,500 lbs and 12,200 lbs. The potential hazards that could lead to an accidental release of the ammonia include, but are not limited to, equipment failure, damage to piping, damage to equipment, human error, etc.\n\t\n\tAccidental Release Prevention Program\n\tAs a part of the OSHA Process Safety Management (PSM) program, Home Market Foods has established numerous programs that serve to prevent accidental rel"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000122388,
      "SummaryText": "eases of ammonia. The programs include:\n\u0095\tProcess Safety Information;\n\u0095\tProcess Hazard Analysis;\n\u0095\tOperating Procedures;\n\u0095\tTraining;\n\u0095\tMechanical Integrity;\n\u0095\tManagement of Change;\n\u0095\tPre Startup Safety Review;\n\u0095\tCompliance Audits;\n\u0095\tThird Party Audits;\n\u0095\tIncident Investigation;\n\u0095\tNatural Hazards and Power Loss;\n\u0095\tFacility Siting;\n\u0095\tCommunity Notification of RMP Accidents;\n\u0095\tEmployee Participation;\n\u0095\tHot Work Permits; and\n\u0095\tContractor Safety.\n\t\n\tEmergency Response Information\n\tAs a part of the PSM Program, Home Market Foods maintains an Emergency Action Plan that was prepared to improve responsiveness to an emergency incident, and to increase the reliability of actions taken during an incident. The plan includes all aspects of emergency response including adequate first aid and medical treatment, evacuations, notification of local emergency response agencies as well as the public. In the event of an emergency involving the ammonia system, it is the policy of the facility to request assistance as necessary by calling 911.\n\t\n\tFive-Year Accident History\n\tThere have not been reportable or non-reportable releases of Ammonia at the Facility within the past five years.\n\t\n\tSteps to Improve Safety\n\tHome Market Foods takes a hands-on approach to ensuring the safety of their employees and community. Home Market Foods will operate and maintain the system in accordance with IIAR Guidance and will continue to implement its OSHA PSM Program in conjunction with the RM Program"
    }
  ]
